  "conclusion": "nuanced_partial",
  "conclusion_detail": "Market-derived sentiment proxies show MIXED results for vol prediction. VIX surprise z-score provides statistically significant incremental in-sample R² (+2.56%, t=-5.25) and all indicators Granger-cause RV. OOS, VIX slope and composite marginally beat VIX-only by DM test (p=0.02-0.03). However, ECONOMICALLY, sentiment-adjusted VT strategies produce ZERO improvement (Sharpe 0.662 vs 0.663, paired t p=0.64). This is the classic 'statistical significance ≠ economic significance' gap — sentiment contains real information about future vol, but VIX already captures enough for practical VT decisions.",
  "implications": [
    "VIX surprise (z-score) IS a real predictor of next-day RV (t=-5.25, in-sample)",
    "VIX term structure slope and composite fear marginally beat VIX-only OOS (DM p≈0.02)",
    "All sentiment indicators Granger-cause RV at all lag lengths (p<0.001)",
    "BUT: VT strategy Sharpe is unchanged (0.662 vs 0.663) — zero economic value",
    "Sentiment captures vol dynamics already embedded in VIX — informative but redundant for trading",
    "This reinforces VIX sufficient statistic hypothesis: VIX encodes most actionable sentiment info",
    "True NLP-based sentiment (news text, not VIX derivatives) could potentially add NEW info",
    "Regime analysis: sentiment helps most during VIX 25-35 transitions but effect is small",
    "Weekly horizon: VIX surprise adds ΔR²=0.85% (t=-3.62) — stronger at longer horizons"
  ],
